  <p>Using a mouse model of gestational drug exposure, we study the independent and combined effects of fentanyl and Δ-9-THC on the placenta, fetal brain, and offspring behavior. This approach allows us to connect early biological changes during pregnancy with later neurodevelopmental outcomes, including motor coordination, activity levels, memory, and social behavior.</p>

  <div class="axis-callout">
    <p><strong>A central focus of this work is the placenta–brain axis.</strong> The placenta is not only a temporary organ that delivers oxygen and nutrients to the fetus; it is also an active communication hub between the mother and the developing baby. It helps regulate immune signals, metabolism, vascular development, hormones, and nutrient transport. My research asks: <strong>what is the placenta sending, or failing to send, to the fetal brain that may contribute to altered neurodevelopment?</strong></p>
  </div>

  <!-- FIGURE 2: Suggested placement for placenta-brain axis schematic -->
