  <p>We also use global and spatial proteomics to better understand how prenatal exposure changes protein expression in the placenta and brain. Global proteomics provides a broad overview of thousands of proteins, while spatial proteomics allows us to map protein changes within specific brain regions and cell types. Together, these methods help us identify which molecular pathways may link placental dysfunction to altered fetal brain development.</p>

  <p>Behavioral testing is used to determine whether these early placental and brain changes are associated with later functional outcomes. Offspring are assessed for motor coordination, spatial memory, activity levels, and social behavior. By integrating placental data, brain structure, molecular profiles, and behavior, this project aims to identify biological pathways that may mediate long-term neurodevelopmental risk.</p>

  <div class="clear-section"></div>

  <h3>Preliminary Findings and Research Direction</h3>

  <p>Our preliminary data suggest that combined prenatal fentanyl and Δ-9-THC exposure disrupts placental gene expression, dampens immune and mitochondrial pathways, impairs vascular development, and reduces placental efficiency. These placental changes are associated with fetal growth restriction, suggesting that the intrauterine environment is altered by prenatal drug exposure.</p>

