  <p>In the fetal brain, early transcriptomic findings point to changes in pathways related to neurotransmitter metabolism, oxidative stress, neuronal development, axon formation, forebrain development, vascularization, myelination, and mitochondrial function. In the postnatal period, offspring exposed to opioids and/or cannabis show behavioral changes such as altered activity, reduced motor coordination, and decreased social preference.</p>

  <p>These findings support the idea that prenatal opioid and cannabis exposure may influence neurodevelopment through both direct effects on the fetal brain and indirect effects mediated by the placenta. A major goal of this project is therefore to identify the placental signals, molecular pathways, and brain-region-specific changes that help explain how early exposure leads to long-term outcomes.</p>

  <div class="clear-section"></div>

  <h3>Why This Matters</h3>

  <p>This research aims to provide a more complete understanding of how prenatal opioid and cannabis exposure affects fetal development. By focusing on the placenta–brain axis, we can move beyond describing outcomes and begin identifying mechanisms. Understanding what the placenta communicates to the fetal brain may help reveal early biomarkers of risk, clarify how combined drug exposure alters development, and support future harm-reduction strategies for pregnancies affected by substance exposure.</p>

  <p>Ultimately, this work seeks to generate knowledge that can inform prenatal care, patient counseling, and public health approaches. By connecting placental biology with fetal brain development and later behavior, this project may help explain why some exposed offspring are more vulnerable to neurodevelopmental impairments and identify biological pathways that could be targeted for future intervention.</p>

